window.docContents[104].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___20-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#chapter-4-section-20":{"contents":"Source: NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §4.20.\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions","header":"4.20. §4.20 Derivatives and Differential Equations","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___20-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#chapter-4-section-20"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms":{"contents":"\\frac{d}{dz}\\ln z=\\frac1z.$$For the principal logarithm, the derivative is 1 / z on Mathlib's slit plane,\nwhich excludes the branch cut and the origin.\n\n\\frac{d}{dz}\\mathrm{Ln}\\,z=\\frac1z.$$Every selected branch of the general logarithm has the same local derivative;\nthe Lean statement records the branch through its HasDerivAt hypothesis.\n\n\\frac{d^n}{dz^n}\\ln z=(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!z^{-n}.$$For a positive integer n, repeated differentiation gives the displayed\nfactorial coefficient and negative power.\n\n\\frac{d^n}{dz^n}\\mathrm{Ln}\\,z=(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!z^{-n}.$$The same higher-derivative formula holds for a general branch wherever its\nfirst derivative is 1 / z; this is a branch-explicit specialization.\n\n\\frac{dw}{dz}=\\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)}.$$For nonvanishing analytic f, this is the logarithmic differential equation\napplied to a candidate w on an explicit domain U; the Lean API records the\nequation as a reusable HasDerivAt predicate rather than asserting it for an\narbitrary function.\n\nw(z)=\\mathrm{Ln}(f(z))+\\text{constant}.$$On an open connected domain, if w and the chosen logarithm branch of f have\nthe same derivative, their difference is one additive constant.  This is a\nlocal-domain statement, as in DLMF.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.7 Derivatives and Differential Equations\u0009§4.7 Derivatives and Differential Equations","header":"§4.7(i) Logarithms","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___14-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___14-Integrals--___6___14-Integrals--Other-integrals":{"contents":"The remaining identities are improper integrals over the positive real ray.\nE4 is the square integral of the canonical positive-real E₁; E5 gives two\nequal values -π/4; E6 gives the two square integrals π/2; and E7 gives the\nmixed canonical Ci–si integral ln 2.  The source values are represented\nby principalCiPositive and principalSi - π/2, and each declaration keeps\nthe improper-integral convergence semantics explicitly.\n\n\\int_0^\\infty E_1^2(t)\\,dt=2\\ln 2$$\nThe square of the canonical positive-real realE1 map has total integral\n2 log 2; its totalized endpoint does not alter the improper integral.\n\n\\int_0^\\infty \\cos t\\,\\operatorname{Ci}(t)\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty \\sin t\\,\\operatorname{si}(t)\\,dt=-\\frac{\\pi}{4}$$\nThe cosine-integral and sine-integral oscillatory products have the same value,\n-π/4.\n\n\\int_0^\\infty \\operatorname{Ci}^2(t)\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty \\operatorname{si}^2(t)\\,dt=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$$\nThe two square integrals are equal and each is π/2.\n\n\\int_0^\\infty \\operatorname{Ci}(t)\\operatorname{si}(t)\\,dt=\\ln 2$$\nThe mixed product has value log 2.\n\nThe source records a historical note that an earlier reference omitted the\nfactor 1/2 in E2.  The displayed E2 formula above includes the corrected\nfactor.\n\nSection 6.14(i) groups E1--E3 as Laplace-transform formulas for the canonical\nexponential, cosine, and sine integrals.\n\nThe source records a sign error in an earlier reference for E7; the displayed\nE7 value here is the corrected positive log 2.\n\nSection 6.14(ii) collects the additional improper integrals E4--E7 over the\npositive real ray.\n\nSection 6.14(iii) points to Apelblat, Bierens de Haan, Erdélyi, and other\nintegral compendia.  These bibliographic pointers add no numbered formula.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.14 Integrals\u0009§6.14 Integrals","header":"Other integrals","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___14-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___14-Integrals--___6___14-Integrals--Other-integrals"},"/Gamma-Function/___5___7-Series-Expansions/#chapter-5-section-7":{"contents":"This page covers every numbered formula in\nDLMF §5.7. Infinite series are stated with HasSum, so convergence\nand the claimed value are one assertion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function","header":"5.7. §5.7 Series Expansions","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___7-Series-Expansions/#chapter-5-section-7"}});